Fix: KV-offloading connector misclassifies Mamba/GDN groups as EAGLE/MTP
draft-model groups, permanently breaking their external-cache lookup.

Full investigation: docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md ("The fix breaks KV
offloading" -> "Root cause found and fixed"). Fast, reliable repro:
bash test_offload_regression.sh (~4 min, works/fails deterministically).

Root cause (two compounding issues in
distributed/kv_transfer/kv_connector/v1/offloading/scheduler.py):

1. SchedulerOffloadConfig.from_spec() has a fallback: if speculative
   decoding is EAGLE-style (MTP counts, via speculative_config.use_eagle())
   and no KV-cache group is explicitly tagged is_eagle_group, it assumes
   *every* group might hold volatile draft-model state and marks them all
   as eagle groups. This model's Mamba/GDN groups aren't tagged
   is_eagle_group (correctly -- they're the main model's own recurrent
   state, not the MTP draft head's; this repo's MTP draft module has no
   mamba layers), so the fallback wrongly swept them in too.

2. Being marked an eagle group adds +1 to the required window size in
   _sliding_window_lookup() (query one extra, provisional chunk, then pop
   it once verified). For Mamba/GDN groups this bumps the lookup from
   needing 1 hit to needing 2 *consecutive* hits. Confirmed via direct
   instrumentation that the actual data structurally never has 2
   consecutive hits available (an artifact of the same eagle-only "extra
   provisional chunk" querying being applied where it doesn't apply) --
   so the lookup was guaranteed to fail every time, at any batch size,
   regardless of any timing/race condition. This is what
   docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md had otherwise fully but incorrectly
   attributed to a free/store timing race (that hypothesis is disproven
   in the doc, kept for the record) after the mamba-align leak fix
   (patches/mamba-align-stale-state-queue.patch) started exercising
   Mamba/GDN offload paths that the original, leakier code rarely
   reached.

A second, smaller, defense-in-depth fix in the same function:
_sliding_window_lookup() scans newest->oldest and previously discarded an
already-confirmed HIT run if *any* newer position it scanned first
returned RETRY (uncertain/still-settling) -- even though that older run
was already safe to use. This is the mirror image of issue 2 above (an
older, valid hit shouldn't be held hostage by whether a newer, unrelated
position has settled yet) and was independently confirmed to matter:
_maximal_prefix_lookup() (used for full-attention groups, scans
oldest->newest) doesn't have this problem since it naturally reaches
"freshest chunks still unsettled" only at the very tail of its range,
where it can just stop cleanly instead of scanning through them first.
Only a HIT_PENDING *within* the actual satisfying run still defers
correctly, since that data genuinely isn't readable yet.

Verified (2026-08-22) with patches/mamba-align-stale-state-queue.patch
also applied, at the default --max-num-batched-tokens 2048 (previously
always failed here -- see the doc's batch-size table): test_offload_
regression.sh passes reliably (multiple runs), 2x concurrent ~88K-token
prompts complete with zero preemptions, and a 3x concurrent ~120K-token
stress test (360K tokens of combined demand against a ~172K-token pool)
completes with zero preemptions, zero discarded/never-offloaded KV cache,
and peak GPU-KV usage of only 72.4%.

